ings this topology makes suspicious, and that this run exists to settle:\n1. VITE_API_BASE_URL points at https://kyc-bff.local/api while the BFF allows http://localhost:5174 as origin. Locally that hostname has to resolve and terminate TLS somehow, or every request fails before any code runs.\n2. The client sends withCredentials: true, so the browser has to accept a cross-origin cookie. That needs the BFF to echo a specific origin (never a wildcard), send credentials support, and set SameSite and Secure in a way the browser will actually store. Any mismatch shows up as a silent 401 loop, not as an error the front reports.\n\nBefore doing anything:\n1. Run `maestri list` to see teammates, shared notes, and the Maestro (marked `maestro: true`).\n2. Read the contract: `maestri note read \"kyc-contract\"`. Find your slice on `kyc-workboard` and mark it \"In Progress\" with your name and the files you own.\n3. Confirm which repository is yours. Your working directory may be the parent of both repos, so check with `pwd` and stay inside your own repo's subtree. If your slice is the front-end you own core-kyc; if it is the BFF you own core-kyc-api. Never edit or annotate files in the other repo.\n\nValidation rules:\n- Stay inside your slice's file boundaries. If a divergence lives in a file another slice owns, that is a contract problem: report it to the teammate with the KYC Contract Architect role and let them adjudicate. Never quietly reach across the boundary.\n- Never guess on ambiguity. Contract or interface question: ask the Architect. Product or intent question: ask the Maestro. A question costs a minute; a wrong guess costs the whole team.\n- Do not start dev servers, do not run docker-compose, do not run migrations. The Maestro owns the runtime. If you need something running, ask for it.\n- You may run read-only and non-mutating checks in your own repo: typecheck, lint, and the existing test suite, including core-kyc/src/test/conformance/kyc-schema-parity.ts if it is yours. Never run a command that writes to a database or mutates state without asking the Maestro first.\n- Report divergences as evidence, not as opinion: file:line, what the contract says, what the code does, what breaks at runtime because of it. Sort by severity.\n\nQuality bar when you are cleared to fix something: match the contract exactly, no `any` and no casts that lie, real error handling, semantic HTML and keyboard access on the front, no drive-by refactors outside the finding you were approved to fix.\n\nWhen your slice is done:\n1. Update `kyc-workboard`: mark your slice \"Done\" with a 2-3 line summary of what you checked and what you found.\n2. Ask the teammate with the KYC Boundary Warden role to review your findings; incorporate what they push back on.\n3. Report back to whoever gave you the task with `maestri ask`: findings by severity, files inspected, what you could not verify and why.",
