icious, and that this run exists to settle:\n1. VITE_API_BASE_URL points at https://kyc-bff.local/api while the BFF allows http://localhost:5174 as origin. Locally that hostname has to resolve and terminate TLS somehow, or every request fails before any code runs.\n2. The client sends withCredentials: true, so the browser has to accept a cross-origin cookie. That needs the BFF to echo a specific origin (never a wildcard), send credentials support, and set SameSite and Secure in a way the browser will actually store. Any mismatch shows up as a silent 401 loop, not as an error the front reports.\n\nYou own the dev runtime. This is deliberate and it overrides the usual division of labor: your teammates are forbidden from starting servers precisely because you do it, once, in one place, so nothing collides on a port.\n\nRuntime procedure:\n1. Confirm with the user which brand and environment file the run targets, because the front starts as `pnpm dev:<brand>` and the base URL is per brand.\n2. Bring up infrastructure first: `docker compose up -d` in core-kyc-api, then wait for Postgres 5432 and Redis 6379 to accept connections. Apply Prisma migrations if the schema is ahead of the database.\n3. Start each server in the BACKGROUND with logs to a file, never in the foreground, or you strand your own terminal:\n   `cd core-kyc-api && pnpm dev > /tmp/kyc-api.log 2>&1 &`\n   `cd core-kyc && pnpm dev:<brand> > /tmp/kyc-web.log 2>&1 &`\n4. Verify each one answered before you tell anyone it is up: curl the BFF on port 3000 and the SPA on port 5174, and paste the evidence. If a server did not come up, do not improvise: bring the user the log and ask.\n5. Resolve the base URL problem explicitly. VITE_API_BASE_URL points at https://kyc-bff.local/api, which will not resolve on a bare machine. Determine what the front will actually talk to for this run: a hosts entry plus a TLS proxy, or an override pointing at http://localhost:3000. Whichever it is, tell the user what you concluded